//! The actor model: message-driven concurrency on top of the async reactor.
//!
//! An actor is a single unit of state that only ever touches its own data and
//! communicates with the outside world by receiving messages one at a time. No
//! two messages for the same actor run at once, so an actor never needs a lock
//! to protect its state: mutual exclusion falls out of the "one message in
//! flight" rule for free. This file is the small, hand-rolled toolkit that makes
//! that pattern work on Kyte's cooperative async runtime.
//!
//! The pieces fit together like this:
//!
//!   * A [`Behavior`] (or [`SupervisedBehavior`]) is the user's code: what to do
//!     with one message of type `M`.
//!   * A [`Mailbox`] is the inbox: an ordered queue of pending messages plus a
//!     signal channel used to wake the actor when work arrives or when it should
//!     stop.
//!   * An [`ActorCell`] glues a mailbox to a behaviour and runs the receive loop
//!     as an async task, draining one message per wake.
//!   * A [`Supervisor`] wraps a fallible behaviour and applies a restart policy,
//!     so a message that fails does not silently kill the actor.
//!   * An [`ActorRegistry`] is an optional name-to-mailbox directory, so actors
//!     can find each other by string name rather than by passing raw handles
//!     around.
//!
//! The core design decision is that the mailbox does NOT ship messages through
//! the channel. The channel (see [`asyncchan`]) only ever carries a one-byte
//! signal: `1` means "a message was enqueued, come and look", `0` means "stop".
//! The actual messages live in an ordinary in-process [`List`], and the actor
//! pops them itself. This keeps the channel payload tiny and fixed-size, and it
//! side-steps having to move arbitrary `M` values through the channel's integer
//! transport. The invariant the loop relies on is that every [`Mailbox.send`]
//! pushes exactly one message and raises exactly one signal, so the count of
//! pending signals never runs ahead of the queue.
//!
//! Everything here runs on the single-reactor async runtime, so "concurrent"
//! means cooperatively interleaved on one thread, not parallel across cores.
//! That is deliberate: the whole point of the actor model here is to get
//! isolation and back-pressure without shared-memory locking. See the wider
//! runtime direction notes in the standard library's concurrency package.

import list;
import map;
import string;
import concurrency.asyncchan;

/// The user-supplied message handler for an actor, generic over message type `M`.
///
/// Implementers provide a single async [`Behavior.receive`] that processes one
/// message. Because it is async, a handler may `await` (do I/O, talk to other
/// actors) without blocking the reactor, and the driving [`ActorCell`] will not
/// pull the next message until the current `receive` completes. This is what
/// gives an actor its "one message at a time" guarantee.
pub trait Behavior<M> {
    /// Handles exactly one message. May `await`; the [`ActorCell`] loop waits
    /// for this to finish before delivering the next message, so state touched
    /// here never races with a second message.
    async fn receive(self, msg: M): void;
}

/// A name-to-mailbox directory so actors can be addressed by string rather than
/// by a raw mailbox handle.
///
/// The value stored per name is a `long`, an opaque mailbox/channel handle (the
/// same kind of handle [`Mailbox.signal`] holds), not a Kyte object reference.
/// Callers register a handle under a well-known name and look it up later,
/// which decouples the sender from however the actor was constructed.
pub struct ActorRegistry {
    /// The backing directory. Maps a registered name to its opaque mailbox
    /// handle. Seeded with capacity 16 and the standard string hash.
    names: map.Map<string, long>,
    /// Creates an empty registry with a small starting capacity.
    init() { self.names = map.Map<string, long>(16, string.hash); }
    /// Records `mbox` under `name`, overwriting any existing entry for that name.
    pub fn register(self: ActorRegistry, name: string, mbox: long): void { self.names.set(name, mbox); }
    /// Returns the handle registered under `name`, or `0` if there is none.
    ///
    /// `0` doubles as the "not found" sentinel, so a caller that might register a
    /// genuine zero handle should use [`ActorRegistry.has`] to disambiguate.
    pub fn lookup(self: ActorRegistry, name: string): long { return self.names.get(name) ?? 0; }
    /// Reports whether `name` is currently registered.
    pub fn has(self: ActorRegistry, name: string): bool { return self.names.has(name); }
    /// Removes the entry for `name` if present; a no-op when it is absent.
    ///
    /// The delete result is intentionally discarded into `_r`: callers that care
    /// whether the name existed should check [`ActorRegistry.has`] beforehand.
    pub fn unregister(self: ActorRegistry, name: string): void { let _r = self.names.delete_key(name); }
    /// Returns how many names are currently registered.
    pub fn count(self: ActorRegistry): int { return self.names.size(); }
}

/// A fallible, restartable message handler used with a [`Supervisor`].
///
/// Unlike [`Behavior`], this receive is synchronous and returns a `bool`
/// success flag rather than throwing: `true` means the message was handled,
/// `false` signals a failure that the supervisor should treat as a fault. A
/// [`SupervisedBehavior.reset`] lets the supervisor restore the handler to a
/// clean starting state before retrying subsequent messages.
pub trait SupervisedBehavior<M> {
    /// Processes one message and returns `true` on success, `false` on failure.
    /// A `false` return is what triggers the supervisor's restart policy.
    fn receive(self, msg: M): bool;
    /// Restores the behaviour to a clean initial state after a failure, called
    /// by the supervisor between a failed message and the next one.
    fn reset(self): void;
}

/// Wraps a [`SupervisedBehavior`] with a bounded restart policy.
///
/// The supervisor counts failures and resets the behaviour after each one, up to
/// `maxRestarts` times. Once that budget is exhausted it latches `stopped` and
/// refuses all further messages, so a persistently failing actor is taken out of
/// service rather than looping on the same fault forever. This is the classic
/// "let it crash, but bound the crashes" supervision idea in miniature.
pub struct Supervisor<M> {
    /// The wrapped handler whose failures this supervisor manages.
    behavior: SupervisedBehavior<M>,
    /// How many times the behaviour has been reset and restarted so far.
    restarts: int,
    /// The maximum number of restarts allowed before the actor is stopped for
    /// good.
    maxRestarts: int,
    /// Latched once the restart budget is spent; blocks all further delivery.
    stopped: bool,
    /// Creates a supervisor around `behavior` that tolerates up to `maxRestarts`
    /// failures before latching stopped. Starts with zero restarts and not
    /// stopped.
    init(behavior: SupervisedBehavior<M>, maxRestarts: int) {
        self.behavior = behavior;
        self.restarts = 0;
        self.maxRestarts = maxRestarts;
        self.stopped = false;
    }

    /// Delivers one message through the restart policy.
    ///
    /// Returns `false` immediately if the actor is already stopped. Otherwise it
    /// calls the behaviour: on success it returns `true`; on failure it either
    /// latches `stopped` and returns `false` (budget exhausted) or resets the
    /// behaviour, bumps [`Supervisor.restartCount`], and returns `true` so the
    /// caller keeps feeding messages. Note the subtlety: a successfully
    /// RECOVERED failure still returns `true`, because from the mailbox loop's
    /// point of view the actor is alive and ready for the next message. Only the
    /// two terminal cases (already stopped, or budget just exhausted) return
    /// `false`.
    pub fn deliver(self: Supervisor<M>, msg: M): bool {
        if (self.stopped) { return false; }
        let ok = self.behavior.receive(msg);
        if (!ok) {
            if (self.restarts >= self.maxRestarts) { self.stopped = true; return false; }
            self.behavior.reset();
            self.restarts = self.restarts + 1;
        }
        return true;
    }

    /// Returns how many times the behaviour has been restarted so far.
    pub fn restartCount(self: Supervisor<M>): int { return self.restarts; }
    /// Reports whether the supervisor has latched stopped and is refusing
    /// messages.
    pub fn isStopped(self: Supervisor<M>): bool { return self.stopped; }
}

/// An actor's inbox: an ordered message queue plus a wake signal.
///
/// Messages are held in an ordinary in-process [`List`], and a separate signal
/// channel is used only to wake the consuming [`ActorCell`]. The channel never
/// carries the message itself, just a one-byte code, so its payload stays small
/// and fixed regardless of `M`. See the file header for why this split exists.
pub struct Mailbox<M> {
    /// Pending messages in arrival order. The producer pushes to the back via
    /// [`Mailbox.send`]; the [`ActorCell`] loop pops from the front.
    pub queue: List<M>,
    /// Opaque handle to the wake channel (see [`asyncchan`]). Carries `1` for
    /// "message enqueued" and `0` for "stop", never a message payload.
    pub signal: long,
    /// Creates an empty mailbox with a fresh wake channel.
    init() {
        self.queue = List<M>();
        self.signal = asyncchan.chanNew();
    }

    /// Enqueues one message and wakes the actor.
    ///
    /// Pushes `msg` to the back of the queue, then sends a `1` signal so the
    /// [`ActorCell`] loop, which may be parked awaiting the channel, wakes and
    /// drains it. The order matters: the message is visible in the queue before
    /// the signal is raised, so the woken loop always finds work waiting.
    pub fn send(self: Mailbox<M>, msg: M): void {
        self.queue.push(msg);
        asyncchan.chanSend(self.signal, 1);
    }

    /// Signals the actor to stop by sending the `0` sentinel on the channel.
    ///
    /// This does not drain or clear the queue; it just tells the running
    /// [`ActorCell.run`] loop to break out on its next wake. Any messages still
    /// queued behind the stop signal are left unprocessed.
    pub fn stop(self: Mailbox<M>): void {
        asyncchan.chanSend(self.signal, 0);
    }
}

/// Binds a [`Mailbox`] to a [`Behavior`] and runs the actor's receive loop.
///
/// The cell is the running instance of an actor: it owns the pairing of "where
/// messages arrive" (the mailbox) and "what to do with them" (the behaviour),
/// and its [`ActorCell.run`] method is the async task that services them one at
/// a time.
pub struct ActorCell<M> {
    /// The inbox this cell drains.
    pub mbox: Mailbox<M>,
    /// The handler invoked for each message pulled from the mailbox.
    pub behavior: Behavior<M>,
    /// Creates a cell that will service `mbox` using `behavior`.
    init(mbox: Mailbox<M>, behavior: Behavior<M>) {
        self.mbox = mbox;
        self.behavior = behavior;
    }

    /// Runs the receive loop until stopped, returning the number of messages
    /// processed.
    ///
    /// The loop parks on the mailbox's signal channel; each wake carries either a
    /// stop code (`0`), which breaks the loop, or a message-ready code, on which
    /// it pops the front message and awaits the behaviour. Because it awaits
    /// `receive` before looping, at most one message is ever in flight, which is
    /// the actor isolation guarantee. Note the loop pulls at most ONE message per
    /// wake and guards with a non-empty check, so a spurious or already-drained
    /// wake is harmless; it relies on [`Mailbox.send`] raising one signal per
    /// enqueued message to keep signals and messages in step. Spawn this as an
    /// async task; it does not return until a stop signal arrives.
    pub async fn run(self: ActorCell<M>): int {
        let processed = 0;
        while (true) {
            let sig = await asyncchan.chanRecv(self.mbox.signal);
            if (sig == 0) { break; }
            if (self.mbox.queue.size() > 0) {
                let msg = self.mbox.queue.at(0);
                self.mbox.queue.remove(0);
                await self.behavior.receive(msg);
                processed = processed + 1;
            }
        }
        return processed;
    }
}
